# One canonical URL for the graph.
#
# /index.html and / both returned 200 with identical content, which splits crawler
# attention and means shared links come in two shapes. Query strings are preserved
# by Netlify, so a shared /index.html?anchor=Mudhoney still lands on the right band.
/index.html   /   301!

# One canonical HOST for the graph.
#
# The custom domain is set as the primary domain in Netlify, and Netlify does
# redirect other custom domains (www) to it — but it keeps serving the project's
# own *.netlify.app subdomain at 200. Verified after the domain launch:
#
#   curl -sSI https://bandmembers.netlify.app/  ->  HTTP/2 200
#
# So the pre-launch address was still serving a byte-identical copy of the site:
# two hosts competing for the same content, every inbound link to the old address
# passing none of its value on, and a canonical tag as the only thing telling
# search engines which one to believe. This makes it an actual redirect.
#
# The hostname must be spelled out in full for a host-level rule; a bare path
# would match every host, including the custom domain, and loop.
#
# Deploy previews and branch deploys are NOT affected: they are served from
# deploy-preview-<n>--bandmembers.netlify.app and <branch>--bandmembers.netlify.app,
# which are different hostnames and do not match these rules. Query strings are
# preserved by Netlify, so /?anchor=KISS survives the hop.
http://bandmembers.netlify.app/*    https://sixdegreesofrock.com/:splat   301!
https://bandmembers.netlify.app/*   https://sixdegreesofrock.com/:splat   301!
